<ContentPage title="The Vision" subtitle="A semi-autonomous community in the Bolivian cloud forest, designed to keep working when the assumptions stop holding.">
  <p>
    BARK -- the Bolivian Ark -- is being built at 1,750 meters in the Yungas, the subtropical seam where the
    Andes collapse into the Amazon basin. The name is deliberate. An ark is not a bunker. It is a vessel
    designed to carry something precious through rough water and land it somewhere worth living.
  </p>

  <h2>A Community, Not a Compound</h2>
  <p>
    The distinction matters. Compounds hoard. Communities trade. BARK runs on five interlocking organs:
    a <strong>fabrication lab and techno park</strong> where appropriate technology gets designed, tested, and
    documented in the open; a <strong>startup incubator</strong> that launches ventures rooted in local capacity;
    a <strong>trading house</strong> that moves Yungas coffee, cacao, and craft goods through channels we control;
    a <strong>retreat center</strong> where traditional Amazonian healing practice meets modern biometric monitoring;
    and the <strong>infrastructure layer</strong> underneath all of it -- solar, spring water, vertical agriculture,
    mesh communications -- that makes autonomy a fact rather than an aspiration.
  </p>
  <p>
    None of these components is novel in isolation. The ambition is in the integration. A maker space without
    its own power grid is a hobby. A retreat center without food sovereignty is a resort. BARK is the wiring
    diagram that connects them into something that actually functions when the road to La Paz washes out.
  </p>

  <h2>Open Source as Operating Principle</h2>
  <p>
    Every solar array schematic, every governance vote, every agricultural trial result gets published. This is
    not generosity -- it is strategy. Open-source projects attract better contributors, survive their founders,
    and resist capture by any single faction. If BARK works, the plans should be worth stealing. We are
    counting on it.
  </p>

  <h2>Embedded, Not Parachuted In</h2>
  <p>
    Coroico has existed since before the Spanish arrived. It does not need saving. What it can use is
    investment that stays, jobs that persist beyond a construction phase, and technology transfer that runs
    in both directions. BARK hires locally, buys locally, and submits its governance to mechanisms that
    give the surrounding community real leverage. The Yungas taught people how to farm vertical terrain
    a thousand years ago. We are here to learn that, not to lecture.
  </p>

  <h2>Resilience Through Capability</h2>
  <p>
    Stockpiling is a losing game -- supplies run out, skills atrophy, morale collapses. BARK invests in
    capability instead. The question is never "how many months of food do we have" but "can we grow, build,
    repair, and teach what we need, indefinitely?" Energy independence, local manufacturing, medical
    knowledge preserved in practice rather than on a shelf. A community that can maintain its own
    systems does not need to predict which crisis comes next.
  </p>

